<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad ibn Ziyad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad ibn Ziyad was the founder of the Ziyadid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. A descendant of the Umayyad governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyad-ibn-abihi">Ziyad ibn Abihi</a>, he was sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> in 818 or 819 to subdue the rebellious tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a>. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> as his base and established a hereditary dynasty that ruled the coastal plain for two centuries, nominally under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>.</p></div>
